# SystemVerilog UART — TX/RX, Flow Control, FIFOs, APB, Formal-Verified

A complete UART transmitter/receiver built from first principles in SystemVerilog:
independently verified TX and RX cores, 16x-oversampled noise-tolerant reception,
FIFO buffering, RTS/CTS flow control, an APB register interface, SVA properties,
and a clean synthesis + timing closure on Intel Cyclone V.

> **Scope note:** verified in simulation (ModelSim) and synthesized +
> timing-closed in Quartus Prime. Not yet run on physical hardware.

## What's inside

### Core UART
- **TX**: 4-module design (FSM, serializer, parity generator, output mux),
  built and verified against an exact port/behavior spec, then re-verified
  against a black-box grading harness (66/66 test cases across 7 phases:
  randomized data at 3 parity modes, injected parity/framing errors,
  back-to-back frames, busy-reject).
- **RX**: mirror-structured (FSM, deserializer, parity checker, edge
  detector), same verification bar.
- **16x oversampling**: a dedicated bit-sampler re-syncs its local counter
  to the detected start edge (not the baud generator's free-running phase),
  samples 3 times near the middle of each bit window, and majority-votes
  the result — verified to reject single-cycle noise glitches and false
  start-bit detections via dedicated tests.
- **Shared baud generator**: one divider instance serving both TX and RX,
  avoiding independent-clock-drift between them.

### System integration
- **FIFOs** on both TX and RX sides, with a real bug found and fixed along
  the way: a first-word-fall-through FIFO racing ahead of a tick-gated
  consumer, silently skipping bytes — caught by a self-checking testbench,
  root-caused, and fixed with a latch at the accept boundary.
- **RTS/CTS flow control**, proven with a two-independent-device testbench
  (a single loopback can't meaningfully test flow control) — including a
  deliberate *negative control* that bypasses flow control to confirm the
  overrun-detection latch genuinely fires when it should.
- **RX overrun detection**, a sticky latch that survives until explicitly
  acknowledged.
- **APB3 register interface** mapping the whole peripheral onto a small
  register set (`TX_DATA`, `RX_DATA`, `STATUS`, `CONTROL`, `OVERRUN_CLR`),
  with proper two-phase SETUP/ACCESS bus timing and `PSLVERR` on invalid
  addresses.

### Verification
- Self-checking SystemVerilog testbenches throughout (queue-based reference
  models, automated PASS/FAIL, not manual waveform inspection).
- Full synthesis, place-and-route, and timing closure in Quartus Prime
  (Cyclone V, `5CSEMA5F31C6`): 0 errors, **positive setup/hold slack across
  all four PVT corners at 50MHz** (worst-case setup slack +16ns of margin
  on a 20ns clock period).

## Architecture

```
uart_top_fifo
├── baud_gen            (shared TX/RX tick generator)
├── uart_top
│   ├── uart_tx
│   │   ├── fsm                  (control FSM)
│   │   ├── Serializer           (parallel -> serial)
│   │   ├── Parity_bit_calc      (parity generation)
│   │   └── mux                  (output select)
│   └── uart_rx
│       ├── fsm_rx                (control FSM, embeds edge_detector)
│       ├── rx_bit_sample         (16x oversample + majority vote)
│       ├── deserializer          (serial -> parallel)
│       └── parity_check          (parity verification)
├── sync_fifo (TX)
└── sync_fifo (RX)

apb_uart_wrapper  →  wraps uart_top_fifo with an APB3 register interface
```

## Register map (APB)

| Offset | Name | R/W | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `0x00` | `TX_DATA` | W | Write a byte — pushes into the TX FIFO |
| `0x04` | `RX_DATA` | R | Read a byte — pops the RX FIFO |
| `0x08` | `STATUS` | R | tx_full, tx_empty, rx_full, rx_empty, tx_busy, rx_busy, rx_parity_err, rx_frame_err, rx_overrun |
| `0x0C` | `CONTROL` | R/W | par_en, par_odd |
| `0x10` | `OVERRUN_CLR` | W | Write 1 to acknowledge/clear the overrun latch |

## A specific bug worth mentioning

One of the trickier bugs in this project: after adding FIFOs, a byte
appeared to "shift" through the TX path — every value came out correct,
just rotated by one position. Root cause: the FIFO's first-word-fall-through
read output raced ahead to the *next* queued byte the instant it was
popped, but the UART's actual data capture didn't happen until a much
later tick-gated edge — by then, the FIFO had already moved on. Fixed by
latching the byte at the exact moment of acceptance, so it stays stable
regardless of how far the FIFO itself advances in the meantime.

## Tooling
- Simulation: ModelSim 
- Synthesis: Quartus Prime (target: Cyclone V, `5CSEMA5F31C6`)
- Coding standard: SystemVerilog only (`logic`, `always_ff`/`always_comb`,
  `case`, async active-low reset)
